/-!
# Invariant function with divisor on an [n]-fibre forces T=0

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.eq_zero_of_forall_transEquiv_eq`
(`73d318fd-3370-5750-925f-852a3b538767`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `b825725e-234e-5db3-a01e-582aba96efd5`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ and $K$ be fields with $K$ an $F$-algebra and $K$ algebraically closed, let $W$ be a
Weierstrass curve over $F$ that is elliptic, and assume the coordinate ring of the base change $W⁄K$
is a Dedekind domain (routine decidability and instance assumptions are grouped here). Let $n$ be a
natural number whose image in $K$ is nonzero. For a nonzero affine point $P$ of $W⁄K$, `placeOf W K
P hP` denotes the height-one prime of the coordinate ring cut out by $P$, namely the ideal generated
by $x-x_P$ and $y-y_P$, with its induced valuation on the function field; and `mulPull W K n`
denotes the $K$-algebra endomorphism of the function field obtained by pulling back along the
multiplication-by-$n$ image of the generic point (the identity if that point fails the genericity
condition `MulGood`). Assume: (i) `hpull`, that for all $P\ne 0$ with $nP\ne 0$, every function $h$
and every $k\in\mathbb N$, if $h$ has valuation at most $\exp(-k)$ at the place of $nP$ then
`mulPull W K n h` has valuation at most $\exp(-k)$ at the place of $P$; (ii) for a point $T$ of
$W⁄K$ and a function $g$, that $g$ has valuation $\exp(-1)$ at the place of every $P\ne 0$ with
$nP\ne 0$ and $nP=T$, and valuation $1$ at the place of every other such $P$; (iii) that $g$ is
fixed by the translation automorphism `transEquiv W K S` for every $S$ with $nS=0$. Then $T=0$.

This is the arithmetic core of the non-degeneracy argument for the Weil pairing (Silverman III.8):
an $E[n]$-invariant function is a pullback along $[n]$, and if its divisor were concentrated on a
single fibre of $[n]$ the maximal ideal of the affine point $T$ would be principal, which is
impossible unless $T=0$. It feeds the non-degeneracy statement
[`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.eq_zero_of_forall_weilPairing0_eq_one`]
(thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.eq_zero_of_forall_weilPairing0_eq_one)
and the construction
[`WeierstrassCurve.exists_pairing_torsionBy`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.exists_pairing_torsionBy) of
the pairing on $n$-torsion.

